  manual standard operating procedure now  clearly the sop  might be able to improved and the  technicians retrained  but such variability and results suggest  that the manual process itself  is not robust enough to deliver the  required levels of quality control  and whilst the technical specifications  of similar particle counting instruments  may appear  equivalent when looking to try to  improve quality control  qc teams should really be looking at the  level of automation in the instrument  which will make the qc testing more  robust and repeatable  now in a light observation counter  there's a light source  typically a laser and which illuminates  through a sampling window in the flow  path  and a detector on the opposite side of  the window  and this detector turns the amount of  light falling on it  into an electrical signal which can be  measured  when particles passing pass through the  measurement window  they partially block the the light  falling onto the sensor  and this reduces the amount of light  falling onto the  detector the change in the detector  electrical output  is proportional to the size of the  particle  flowing through the uh the laser light  now the attraction of this method is  that the counter can be calibrated using  certified traceable calibration beads of  a known size  these beads will obscure a fixed or  certain amount of light  and now any particle however irregular  shaped it is  which obscures the same amount of light  as the calibration beads  can be said to have an equi equivalent  spherical diameter  as the calibration beads thus irregular  shaped contaminating particles may be  sized  according to certified restaurant  reference standards  and no matter what their shape particles  that obscure the same amount of light  will be reported as being the same  equivalent spherical diameter  now this helps almost eliminate the  impact of subjectivity and human error  from the measurement  okay so now you can understand why the  us